title op3: duplicate content leaves the target ambiguous

# The unique-target guard, and the reason the withdrawn
# cross-directory fingerprint search had to go.
#
# Off-of deletes /src and creates two directories, each holding one
# file with the same bytes the deleted file had, under a different
# leaf.  Onto is quiet.
#
# Both new directories reach the docket the same way: /src is a
# deletion and /x and /y are additions among the siblings of the
# green-corresponded root, and the sibling generator pairs a deletion
# with each addition.  Note that a single deletion facing two
# additions produces two candidates, not a choice -- the generator
# nominates and judges nothing, which is what makes phase 1 monotone.
#
# The one voter, f, is severed AND leaf-renamed, so neither red nor a
# name match is available to it and it must vote by yellow, the row
# of the triad that survives a rename and a sever.  Its bytes sit in
# both /x and /y, so inside each candidate pair the sole voter is
# accounted for.  Two targets are equally good.
#
# Belief requires a unique target, so nothing is concluded, and the
# decay is the honest one: /src dies, /x and /y are born.  The same
# ambiguity stops pairing from adopting either file, so the two new
# files keep their new dnodes.
#
# This is precisely the failure the fingerprint search would have
# papered over by picking one, and picking one here would be a guess
# presented as a decision.  The theory's own phrase for the case
# where identity and name evidence are both dead is that the answer
# is "novel," not a guess.
#
# Hand-worked: clean, both new directories present, nothing reused.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: src/
      3-100: f = dup

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: src/
      3-100: f = dup

tree offof
  1-1: /
    10-300: x/
      11-300: g = dup
    12-300: y/
      13-300: h = dup

#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none.  /src has TWO candidates, /x and /y, and
#                    its single voter is accounted for in both, so
#                    the unique-target guard blocks
#   deltas:          none
#   decisions:       /src and its f die; /x, /y and their files are
#                    born.  Nothing is reused, because the same
#                    ambiguity stops pairing adopting either file
#   conflicts:       none, onto is quiet
#   emission:        unlink f then unlink src; link x, g, y and h
# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    10-300: x
      11-300: g = dup
    12-300: y
      13-300: h = dup
